NNTP-Posting-Date: 28 Jun 2000 01:55:48 GMT
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha


We are in the market for a Beowulf class cluster and have been comparing
Alpha and PIII based clusters. Our primary application is Monte Carlo
photon transport problems (e.g., using codes like MCNP). We also have
a number custom codes. We have compared an 667 MHz XP1000 running linux
with the compaq fortran compilers to a Coppermine PIII 700 MHz with 100
MHz SDRAM. We find that the advantage of the Alpha ranges from 40-100%. I
have tried a variety of compilation options including -fast and various
levels of -O and this is the best result I get for the Pentium. The extra
speed for the alpha is nice, but the price difference is ~5 to 1 for a
dual CPU UP2000 compared to a dual PIII ! I am surprised that the speed
difference is this small. Given the disparity in the price-performance,
we are planning on buying the Pentiums. Do these speed differences seem
reasonable? Part of the problem is that the Alpha seems to have been
stuck at ~667 MHz for about a year while the Pentium speeds have doubled.
API has been promising 1 GHz for quite a while, but I see nothing. It seems
that they are starting to fall a little too far behind the price performance
curve.

=======================================================================
Eric C. Frey 
Assistant Professor 	
Department of Biomedical Engineering and Department of Radiology
The University of North Carolina



