Holger van Koll <holger@vankoll.de> writes:

>I got a 150mhz Jensen and a 166Mhz Pentium.

So you have a 1992 Alpha, and a 1995 Intel CPU.

>When running the distributed.net client or compiling a kernel,
>the pentium(!) is 60% to 100% faster.

See above. Oh, and the oft repeated "RC5 is a 32 bit rotate based cypher,
whereas the Alpha is a 64 bit rotate-less architecture, meaning that you
need many more instructions to do an RC5 iteration. I know, I programmed
the RC5 routines in the linux/AXP client" sermon.

Now go and do a DES benchmark (DES, due to bitslicing, actually profiting
from the 64-bitness of the Alpha, not to mention from the many many more
general purpose registers).

>Could somebody explain that to me? Is it just floatingpoint/integer
>related?

Not really. The stack based x86 FPU architecture pretty much sucks, so
even a 2-3 years older Alpha chip will probably be competitive with that
1995 x86; The x86 integer stuff, while far (very far) from perfect, isn't
quite as bad, so you might need a slightly more contemporary Alpha to
keep up.

