Wow, great questions...  Here's my ideas:

Regarding PowerPC floating point, I see a reasonably good advantage
for PPC floating point over x86.  It may have more to do with having
a target-optimized math library (versus the stock, run-anywhere one
that comes with Linux/x86) and more registers than what types of
instructions can be executed on the machine.  But those differences
are pretty significant.  Having the capability to do a fused
multiply/ add on both machines doesn't mean that they will both be
capable of actually feeding the instructions, for example.  Since
there's only 8 real floating point registers on x86, and the multiply
pipeline is about as long, I don't think you'd be able to sustain a
matrix multiplication without stalling the pipeline.  On an alpha
this isn't a problem (thanks Goto-san!) but on an x86 box I've never
seen decent MFLOP numbers.

About the last question, this is the first I've heard of making a
distinction between CISC and RISC in terms of parallelizability. Off
the top of my head, I can't think of a reason why there would be a
difference per se.  About the only other CISC machine available
nowadays is an S/390 and they have pretty decent memory bandwidth and
scale fairly well (he he :) so I'm not sure there is such an effect. 
Being able to stream more data through a RISC CPU may actually hurt
SMP performance because of resource contention. Small SMP RISC and
CISC systems have a few design differences like memory bus width and
cache size so I'm not sure a direct comparison of this type between
systems is possible.  I'd be surprised if just the general CPU type
was a factor.

Luckily, Compaq is providing a nice place to run benchmarks so we
can see how our codes run.  I personally get a very large price/
performance advantage from Alphas, so that's what I use.

I hope other people can post more useful responses than this... =-]

Regards,

Tony Hammitt



