Hello Erik!

E> I'm wondering if anyone has available an image of the Jensen config
E> utility disk to set things back to the way they need to be for Win
NT

You shouldn't need it, just get to the SRM prompt (this is the blue
screen showing ">>> ", you can stop the boot process by pressing the
reset button on the case) and type "set os_type 1" and then turn the
power off and on again. After that you should find a menu driven setup
screen. From here, you can install NT, if you need further
instructions, send an email. But beware, the Jensen is rather slow with
NT, like something between an old P90 and a P133 with half the memory
of you Jensen depending on the applications you intend to run.

You should consider to install FX!32 which allows you to run most x86
Windows applications, but this tends to be really slow. You should
configure it not to optimize while the application is running, and not
shut an application down before you finished working. The optimizer
should run only when you do not work, because it eats up all CPU power,
and you should let it run until it's finished before you shut down NT,
because it would continue the next time when you start working.
(Perhaps you can configure it to start on demand only, I don't
remember.)

If you want to run both Linux/OSF1/DU/Tru64/VMS and NT on your Jensen,
you MUST have at least two physical SCSI disks, one with BSD disklabel,
and one with NT fdisk partitioned. When Linux is running, however, you
can mount almost any partition on any disk installed (well, at least
most fs drivers compile, I tested FAT16 and VFAT16 and UMSDOS only, my
kernel has no NTFS support yet and I had no other disks to play around
with.)

But, honestly, any Jensen should run Linux nowadays; for NT (if you are
considering to install NT4, at least), it's probably the second worst
choice after an x86 inferior to a P90 or an AMD DX4/100.

Viele Gruesse, Christoph




