Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

Hallo Michael!

MK> _If_ the CDs are bootable for the Jensen, a 'boot dkaX00' from the
SRM
MK> console should help.

_And_ _if_ the CD drive can be switched to a block size of 512 bytes
(most of them use 2048.)

MK> IMHO there are some mistakes in this manual:
MK> [...]
>> 7.Enter your partitions. The first partition has to be a special
ext2
>> partition only for booting, i.e. containing the kernel(s), 8MB is
enough for
>>      that. The rest is at your choice.
MK> [...]
MK> For the installation with aboot, the first partition is not gonna
be an
MK> ext2 partition (minlabel.exe has no means of initializing one), its
MK> partition table entry is just needet to reserve disk space for the
MK> compressed kernel image written to the disk behind the aboot code.
The
MK> jensenX.img disks do contain such an image of aboot & kernel.

You just need the free space necessary for aboot and the compressed
kernel, 2 MB should be enough for that purpose. You don't need any
partition table entry for this free space, so I prefer to start the
first partition at an offset of 2 "cylinders" after the disk's
beginning. So it can't be accidentally overwritten with an fs or
"mounted", which would fail, of course, and can result in a crash.

MK> Caveat: After a successful installation, _never_ create and
initialize a
MK> partition starting at the beginning of the disk: The code of aboot
MK> doesn't fit to the bootblock, so some 100 KB of the space at the
MK> beginning of the disk are occupied by it, even without a subsequent
MK> kernel image. [...]

This is absolutely correct.

MK> _Never_ try to create a filesystem on partition 1.

_If_ partition 1 starts right at the beginning of the disk. If you
leave some space free before the first partition, you can use it
safely, of course.

Regards, Christoph



