Hi there...

On Thu, 22 Jun 2000, Stephen S. wrote:

> I have three questions I would like to ask.  First, I have Redhat 6.2
> up and running.  When I go into X and open up a konsole (kde) it opens
> a dummy console and then another one.  Anyone ever seen this before.
> I tried creating a new link on the desktop but it still happens.  I
> even tried opening a rxvt window.  Same thing.  When I do a ps -aux
> from the window it shows konsole open and running (spawning) another
> konsole session.  Wierd.  This is probably a setting somewhere.

It seems like your KDE is running thins 'in terminal'. As root open up
your kfm, then Edit->Global Applications->Utilities. Right click on the
'konsole.kdelink' file, get Properties up and select the 'Execute'
tab. You should read 'konsole %i %mi -caption "%c"' or so in the 'Execute'
field (on the left of the 'Browse' button) and nothing else. The 'run in
terminal' option should be checked off.

> Next.  I have a problem with my modules.  Apon boot anything that
> requires a modeule to load will fail (ethernet, sound or fs support)
> It seems that the path pointing to the module locations is screwed up,
> or atleast that's what is seems to me.  It has "BOOT" on the end of
> the directory name pointing to the modules.  I was wondering where I
> could look to find out where the module location is set to.

I guess of course that your kernel supports modules and/or autoloading. If
everything is ok with these, then check out the module-info file in /boot
directory. Deleting this file and it's corresponding symlink in the same
dir should be safe, provided you have a System.map file in /boot or
/usr/src/linux. If everything is ok, check that you have the modules for
your kernel installed under /lib/modules. There should be a directory for
your exact kernel version (as in 2.2.16pre7 for a 2.2.16pre7 kernel. a
2.2.16 dir won't do, not to mention a 2.2.14BOOT directory for a kernel
named 2.2.14 and NOT 2.2.14BOOT). The module location is deduced from the
kernel version and is set to /lib/modules/<exact kernel version>. If you
are recompiling your kernel, remember to first wipe everything out of the
corresponding directory in /lib/modules/<kernel version> before the 'make
modules_install' part.

> Last.  I can get milo to boot (from the Alpha BIOS) but I cannot boot
> linux from milo without using the floppy.  I have a /boot partition on
> hda3.  It has the newest compiled kernel, the one I want to use, but
> it won't load.  The error is simply "Failed to load kernel".  Also
> when I do an ls of hda3 from milo, it looks like  a bunch of % signs,
> not the files.

Make sure that hda3 is an ext2-formatted filesystem and that the file
parameter you pass to milo (hda3:/boot/vmlinux.gz) is the gzipped file
itself and not a symlink to it. AFAIK most or all off-the-self milos
don't support symlinks. If hda3 is a dos-formatted partition, place the
kernel in the root directory and then 'boot hda3:vmlinux.gz'. BTW don't
forget any 'video=...' parameters if you have frame-buffer support
compiled in your kernel.
 
Hope i helped...

See ya,
Nick.

