Tom Sevy (tsevy@nospam.echeck.com) wrote:
: Also, I don't think I saw anyone address the benefit of using a 64-bit OS
: and 64-bit Compiler vs. 32-bit OS and 32-bit Compiler.

Using a 64-bit (or n-bit, for any value of n) compiler and OS does not 
confer a performance advantage *by itself*. For some problems, 64-bit
addressing and/or efficient support for 64-bit integers may help; for
others it may hurt, by decreasing the amount of (larger) data items 
fitting into cache; and for many problems limited by floating point
calculations 64-bits vs. 32-bits makes no difference at all.

In this respect, it is quite interesting to look at the optimization
flags used in vendor's SPEC submissions: In the Compaq DS20 peak SpecFp
submission, two (out of 14) tests were compiled with -taso flag, 
effectively making pointers 32-bit in size. For the SpecInt submission,
-taso was used three times (out of 12). I do not think Compaq would
have used it unless it helped performance ;-)

/Serge.P

