I can report the same kind of problem.

I'm running 2 dual 21264 machines.  One NFSs its /usr/local and home dir off
the other.

I'm using a myrinet setup using ssh as the loginer for mpich code.  However,
this seems to be a problem since I get:

open.c:43 spinlock stuck in sshd at fffffc0000342ee0(0) owner xauth at
ffffc00003217a4(1) sched.c:30.

I actually got this IDENTICAL message repeatedly after the first required me
to reboot the machine.

If you guys report the *actual* message it would help.

In my case, this clearly is telling me that the sched.c code used by xauth,
called by sshd using open.c (or visa versa perhaps) is trying to open the
same file on both my machines.  If NFS fails to lock exactly right, then a
CPU will get confused by what's going on.

The solution for me was to call ssh -a -x instead...Thereby avoiding each
CPU making/locking a .Xauthority file, in the same NFSed home directory.

This is probably an NFS problem, but at the core the kernel is broken as it
shouldn't kill the system just becaues NFS lies.

This was a bitch of a problem because the lock is so hard.  If there are any
real kernel coders, we need your help!

Thanks,
