Toon Moene <toon@moene.indiv.nluug.nl> writes:

> On the comp-fortran-90 mailing list
> we noticed, when discussing a SPEC2000 Fortran entry, that apparently:
> 
>       INTEGER(8)
> 
> slipped through.  Hardly a Standard way of specifying a certain size
> INTEGER ...

You'll notice something similar in facerec, where all REALs are declared as
REAL(4). Initially, all INTEGERs where INTEGER(4) as well...until that lead to
some problems with the Cray compiler (for which default types are equivalent
to KIND=8). In part, this is due to my inexperience with F90's KIND system
when writing the code. Also, SPEC had enough problems sorting out the various
portability and correctness issues (including catering to compilers with
borderline cases of optimitis) than to write and enforce a style guide for the
three languages involved - that's how I see it from the outside as a concerned
party, as it were.

When confronted with this issue, the CPU subcommittee agreed with leaving
the REAL declaration as is, making all integers of default type, and inserting
some explicit type conversions and constant kind declarations where necessary.
This is a thorny issue due to the implicit and varying actual type the default
types are mapped to. Also, I think there is only one compiler on the market
that uses KIND=<log2 (length in bytes)> instead of the usual KIND=<length in
bytes>, and its vendor is not represented in SPEC (not that that matters any).

	Jan



