The benchmarks that use taso though are C, and the title of this 
thread doesn't ask about C performance so your SPEC2K example, 
while amusing, doesn't apply unless the FORTRAN progam under 
question, happens to use lots of pointers. Since I didn't
write it, I'll defer to the author of the code to decide if the
taso flag is useful in his case.

Back to the original question.

It would probably be useful to use profiling tools to determine
exactly what portion of the code is hogging cpu cycles so that
one can look at the offending code to see what it's problem is.
Things like gprof come to mind. At least then you'll have some
understanding of where to look. The idea of statically linking
under Tru64 and running on Linux is also a good idea as mentioned
previously. The linux-kernel mailing list has a fair discussion 
of page coloring and patches floating around from folks working 
on it. Also, there was a math library problem discussed recently
where the library was generating huge volumes of minor page faults.
You might want to do "time bnd" and see if that's hitting you.
Look for many thousands of minor faults.


Wes



