As a member of the SPEC CPU committee, I have first-hand experience in
the perverseness of this particular group.  (Though I was not on the committee
when this issue was being worked on.)

"Perverse" was probably not the best word.   The standards committee that 
created the Fortran90 language (X3J3) worked really hard to create a portable
way of specifying precision.  They recognized the dangers of "partial
standardization" (which is pretty much what has happened), but were unable
to craft a set of rules that would discourage it strongly enough....

I expected that vendors would also recognize those dangers and try harder
to make the new utilities easy to use.  The present situation encourages
people to use non-standard features without realizing that they are non-
standard, and I suspect that in the long run this is more expensive for
everyone....

So, to answer your question, it is the vendors who are perverse for exposing
implementation-dependent constants to the customers and thus implying that
there is some degree of portability in the use of these constants.  My own
employer is guilty of this as well, at least in the XLF Fortran v6.1 Reference
Manual that I just looked this up in....
-- 
