Subject: graphics upgrade for AlphaStation 200 ?
Date: 2000/06/07
Message-ID: <johnc-E4CE9D.21551107062000@news.tulane.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 632443596
Organization: Tulane University
User-Agent: MT-NewsWatcher/3.0 (PPC)
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

For years, our lab has owned an AlphaStation 200 4/233 (with SRM 
console, FWIW). Although it runs fine, it has not seen much use because 
it is rather poorly-outfitted in the graphics department -- a15" monitor 
plugged into a DEC ZLXp-E1 (DC21030-based, "TGA") video card. We would 
like to use the AS200 as an X workstation, but the crappy video 
capabilities of the graphics card and monitor (only 8-bit at 832x624) 
have rendered it basically useless for our purposes. 

We are planning to upgrade to a 19" monitor and a decent graphics card 
that can that support up to 1280x1024 resolution in 16 or 24 bit color 
with some 2-D and 3-D hardward acceleration and (if possible) OpenGL/GLX 
support. Ideally, we would like to use Compaq's "True64" OS (already 
have a license for it), but from our limited information it seems to 
only support a few high-end graphics cards that all seem out of our 
price range ($650 and up). Can anyone tell me if there are any cheaper, 
VGA-based graphics cards (e.g., ATI Mach 64) that are supported by 
True64 on this machine?

Our alternative to the expensive DEC-video/True64 solution seems to be 
some type of cheap (sub $250) VGA graphics card with BSD or Linux. 
Apparently both Linux and NetBSD support the Alpha with a number of VGA 
cards (Matrox, ATI, S3, Voodoo?), but we can't figure out which 
combination would be right for us. Can anyone point us to a particular 
*nix/VGA combo to meet the following needs:

(a) high-res video at 16 or 24 bpp color; possible OpenGL/Mesa support
(b) stability & ease of installation of OS
(c) cost and availability of graphics card

We would *greatly* appreciate any comments and suggestions on our choice 
of OS (True64 vs. Linux vs. various BSDs) and video card (DEC vs. 
various PCI/VGA).

_________________________________________________________________________
John C. Coleman, Ph.D. Candidate          Dept. of Biomedical Engineering
johnc@tulane.edu                          Tulane University
http://www.bmen.tulane.edu/~johnc/        New Orleans, LA USA



