Hi Holger, hi Mike.

Holger van Koll wrote:
> Mike wrote:
> > I managed it to the 'aboot>' prompt!
> > If I do 'show dev' on OSF console, shouldn't I see my cd-rom device?

Yes, it should be there as dkaX00, X being its SCSI - ID.

> > What now? I have Suse Linux CDs...

_If_ the CDs are bootable for the Jensen, a 'boot dkaX00' from the SRM
console should help.

> I had a look at
> http://www.unix-ag.org/Linux-Alpha/Architectures/Jensen_install.html and
> "installed" Linux this way.

IMHO there are some mistakes in this manual:
[...]
> 7.Enter your partitions. The first partition has to be a special ext2 partition only for booting, i.e. containing the kernel(s), 8MB is enough for
>      that. The rest is at your choice.
[...]
For the installation with aboot, the first partition is not gonna be an
ext2 partition (minlabel.exe has no means of initializing one), its
partition table entry is just needet to reserve disk space for the
compressed kernel image written to the disk behind the aboot code. The
jensenX.img disks do contain such an image of aboot & kernel.

Caveat: After a successful installation, _never_ create and initialize a
partition starting at the beginning of the disk: The code of aboot
doesn't fit to the bootblock, so some 100 KB of the space at the
beginning of the disk are occupied by it, even without a subsequent
kernel image.

[...]
> 12.On the aboot prompt enter EXACTLY like shown:
>      1/vmlinux.gz load_ramdisk=1 root=/dev/fd0H1440 
[...]
As mentioned above, minlabel.exe and cobyboot.exe won't produce a valid
first partition, so '1/vmlinux.gz' cannot work. It should be
'0/[filename]' (where [filename] does not matter) to load the compressed
kernel image right behind aboot.

> if I change 1/vmlinux.gz to 0/vmlinux.gz I get
> 
> booted_dev...(like above) , guessing boot_devce=`scd0`
> loading compressed vmlinux.gz
> unz8ip: unknown compression method
> loading plain vmlinux.gz
> unexpected object file format 0

Which image do you use?
The only one that worked for my jensen was [1].

> My "linux-hd" is sdc , is scd0 a typo?!?

Nope, as stated, aboot guesses the boot device to be scd0 (CD ROM). :-)
As far as this information is not used for anything, it shouldn't bother
you.

> I have 8mb boot partition as partition 1 , 900mb extfs as 2 , 70mb swap
> as 3

_Never_ try to create a filesystem on partition 1.

> What does 1/vmlinux.gz mean? Load /vmlinux.gz from partition 1 ??

See [2].

[1] ftp://ftp.lur.rwth-aachen.de/jensen/jensen3.img
[2] http://www.alphalinux.org/faq/srm-4.html

hth & Greets
  Michael Keller
